narrative:
## Two Guards at Two Different Gates

XIOM has two independent safety systems that work at different levels:

### Ownership Prevents MEMORY Errors
```xiom
fn bad_move() -> Int {
  let data = Vec::new();
  data.push(42);
  let stolen = data;  // 'data' moved to 'stolen'
  return data.len();  // COMPILE ERROR: use after move
}
```
The borrow checker stops this BEFORE the program runs. No segfault, no garbage data, no silent corruption.

### Contracts Prevent LOGIC Errors
```xiom
type Account = {
  balance: Float64;
  invariant: balance >= 0.0;
}

fn bad_transfer(from: &mut Account, to: &mut Account, amt: Float64) {
  from.balance = from.balance - amt;
  to.balance = to.balance + amt;
  // No requires — amt could be ANY value!
}
```
If `amt` is negative, you're stealing money. If `amt > from.balance`, `from` goes negative. Contracts catch these at runtime (or with Z3, at compile time).

### The Beautiful Overlap
```xiom
fn safe_transfer(from: &mut Account, to: &mut Account, amt: Float64)
  requires: amt > 0.0;
  requires: amt <= from.balance;
  ensures: from.balance == from@pre.balance - amt;
  ensures: to.balance == to@pre.balance + amt;
{
  from.balance = from.balance - amt;
  to.balance = to.balance + amt;
}
```
Ownership guarantees no one can modify `from` or `to` during the transfer. Contracts guarantee the transfer is mathematically sound. Neither could catch all bugs alone — together, they're nearly airtight.

### The Remaining Gap
What contracts AND ownership can't catch: incorrect algorithms, off-by-one in loop logic, business rule misunderstandings. That's what testing is for.

